"To find the positive difference between \\(120\\%\\) of 30 and \\(130\\%\\) of 20, we first need to calculate each percentage separately.\n\n1. Calculate \\(120\\%\\) of 30:\n   \\[\n   120\\% \\text{ of } 30 = \\frac{120}{100} \\times 30 = 1.2 \\times 30 = 36\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(130\\%\\) of 20:\n   \\[\n   130\\% \\text{ of } 20 = \\frac{130}{100} \\times 20 = 1.3 \\times 20 = 26\n   \\]\n\n3. Find the positive difference between the two results:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Positive difference} = |36 - 26| = 10\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the positive difference is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $10$. I hope it is correct."
"To find the 100th term of the arithmetic sequence 6, 10, 14, 18, ..., we need to use the formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:\n\n\\[ a_n = a + (n-1)d \\]\n\nwhere \\(a\\) is the first term, \\(d\\) is the common difference, and \\(n\\) is the term number.\n\nGiven:\n- The first term \\(a\\) is 6.\n- The common difference \\(d\\) is 4.\n\nWe need to find the 100th term, so \\(n = 100\\).\n\nSubstitute the values into the formula:\n\n\\[ a_{100} = 6 + (100-1) \\cdot 4 \\]\n\\[ a_{100} = 6 + 99 \\cdot 4 \\]\n\\[ a_{100} = 6 + 396 \\]\n\\[ a_{100} = 402 \\]\n\nTherefore, the 100th term of the arithmetic sequence is 402."
"To solve the equation \\( |5x - 1| = |3x + 2| \\), we need to consider the properties of absolute values. The absolute value equation \\( |A| = |B| \\) implies that either \\( A = B \\) or \\( A = -B \\).\n\nFirst, we solve the equation \\( 5x - 1 = 3x + 2 \\):\n\n\\[\n5x - 1 = 3x + 2\n\\]\n\\[\n5x - 3x = 2 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n2x = 3\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve the equation \\( 5x - 1 = -(3x + 2) \\):\n\n\\[\n5x - 1 = -3x - 2\n\\]\n\\[\n5x + 3x = -2 + 1\n\\]\n\\[\n8x = -1\n\\]\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{1}{8}\n\\]\n\nThe smallest value of \\( x \\) from these solutions is \\( \\boxed{-\\frac{1}{8}} \\)."
"To find the midpoint of a segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\), we use the midpoint formula:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the endpoints \\((1, 4)\\) and \\((1, 10)\\), we substitute these coordinates into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1 + 1}{2}, \\frac{4 + 10}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expressions:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{2}{2}, \\frac{14}{2} \\right) = (1, 7)\n\\]\n\nThe coordinates of the midpoint are \\((1, 7)\\). To find the sum of the coordinates, we add these coordinates together:\n\n\\[\n1 + 7 = 8\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the coordinates of the midpoint is \\boxed{8}.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $8$. I hope it is correct."
"Given the equation \\(\\frac{A\\sqrt{B}}{C} = \\frac{9}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\), we can equate the numerators and denominators separately:\n\n\\[\nA\\sqrt{B} = 9 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad C = 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(A\\sqrt{B} = 9\\), we can solve for \\(A\\):\n\n\\[\nA = \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{B}} = 9\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(C = 2\\sqrt{3}\\), we can solve for \\(C\\):\n\n\\[\nC = 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) have no common prime factors, and \\(B\\) has no perfect-square factors other than 1. Therefore, \\(A = 9\\), \\(B = 3\\), and \\(C = 2\\).\n\nTo find \\(A + B + C\\):\n\n\\[\nA + B + C = 9 + 3 + 2 = 14\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(A + B + C\\) is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).}"
