"We can model the altitude of the airplane as an arithmetic sequence where the first term $a = 100$ feet and the common difference $d = 100$ feet. We want to find the number of terms $n$ such that the sum of the first $n$ terms of this sequence is 12,000 feet.\n\nThe sum of the first $n$ terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by the formula:\n\n$$S_n = \\frac{n}{2} (2a + (n-1)d)$$\n\nWe want to find $n$ such that $S_n = 12000$. Plugging in the values for $a$ and $d$, we get:\n\n$$12000 = \\frac{n}{2} (2(100) + (n-1)(100))$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$12000 = \\frac{n}{2} (200 + 100n - 100)$$\n\n$$12000 = \\frac{n}{2} (100n + 100)$$​"
