"We need to evaluate \\(3 \\star 11\\) using the given operation \\(a \\star b = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{b} - \\dfrac{1}{a}\\right)}{(a - b)}\\).\n\nFirst, substitute \\(a = 3\\) and \\(b = 11\\) into the operation:\n\n\\[\n3 \\star 11 = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{11} - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)}{(3 - 11)}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the numerator:\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{1}{11} - \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{3 - 31}{33} = \\dfrac{-28}{33}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the denominator:\n\n\\[\n3 - 11 = -8\n\\]\n\nNow, divide the simplified numerator by the simplified denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\dfrac{\\dfrac{-28}{33}}{-8} = \\dfrac{28}{264}\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(3 \\star 11 = \\boxed{\\dfrac{28}{264}}\\)."
"Given the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + bx + c = 0\\), we know that the difference of the roots is \\(|b - 2c|\\). Let the roots of the equation be \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\). According to Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots \\(\\alpha + \\beta\\) is equal to \\(-b\\), and the product of the roots \\(\\alpha \\beta\\) is equal to \\(c\\).\n\nThe difference of the roots is given by:\n\\[|\\alpha - \\beta| = \\sqrt{(\\alpha + \\beta)^2 - 4\\alpha\\beta} = \\sqrt{(-b)^2 - 4c} = \\sqrt{b^2 - 4c} = |b - 2c|.\\]\n\nSince \\(c \\neq 0\\), we can equate the expressions:\n\\[\\sqrt{b^2 - 4c} = b - 2c.\\]\n\nSquaring both sides, we get:\n\\[b^2 - 4c = (b - 2c)^2.\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(\\boxed{b^2 - 4c = (b - 2c)^2}.\\]"
