"To solve the equation \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{25}\\right)^{x + 2} = 125^{-x}\\), we can rewrite both sides with the same base. Notice that \\(125 = 25^2\\), so \\(125^{-x} = (25^2)^{-x} = 25^{-2x}\\).\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1}{25}\\right)^{x + 2} = 25^{-2x}\n\\]\n\nSince the bases are the same, we can set the exponents equal to each other:\n\\[\nx + 2 = -2x\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(x\\) by isolating \\(x\\) on one side of the equation. First, add \\(2x\\) to both sides:\n\\[\nx + 2x + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n3x + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n3x = -2\n\\]\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{2}{3}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the solution is \\(\\boxed{x = -\\frac{2}{3}}\\)."
"To find the least possible value of the sum \\( |x - 1| + |x - 1.5| + |x - 2| \\), we need to consider the properties of the absolute value function. The sum of the absolute values of \\( x \\) from the points 1, 1.5, and 2 represents the total distance from \\( x \\) to these three points on the number line.\n\nThe minimum value of this sum occurs when \\( x \\) is exactly at the midpoint of the interval [1, 1.5, 2]. The midpoint of this interval is the average of 1, 1.5, and 2:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Midpoint} = \\frac{1 + 1.5 + 2}{3} = \\frac{4.5}{3} = 1.5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the least possible value of the sum \\( |x - 1| + |x - 1.5| + |x - 2| \\) is achieved when \\( x = 1.5 \\).\n\nThus, the minimum value of the sum is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1.5}\n\\]"
