"To determine the smallest possible integer value for \\( x \\) such that \\( f(x) = \\frac{\\sqrt{2x-6}}{x-3} \\) has a real number value, we need to ensure that the expression inside the square root is non-negative and the denominator is not zero.\n\n1. The expression inside the square root is \\( 2x - 6 \\). For the square root to be real, \\( 2x - 6 \\geq 0 \\).\n   \\[\n   2x - 6 \\geq 0 \\implies 2x \\geq 6 \\implies x \\geq 3\n   \\]\n\n2. The denominator is \\( x - 3 \\). For the fraction to be defined, \\( x - 3 \\neq 0 \\).\n   \\[\n   x - 3 \\neq 0 \\implies x \\neq 3\n   \\]\n\nCombining these two conditions, we get:\n\\[\nx \\geq 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x \\neq 3\n\\]\n\nThe smallest integer value that satisfies both conditions is \\( x = 4 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]"
