"To determine the number of three-element sets \\(\\{a, b, c\\}\\) of distinct positive integers such that \\(a \\times b \\times c = 2310\\), we start by finding the prime factorization of 2310.\n\n\\[\n2310 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5 \\times 7 \\times 11\n\\]\n\nThe prime factors of 2310 are 2, 3, 5, 7, and 11. We need to find the number of ways to choose 3 distinct factors from these 5 factors. This is a combination problem, and we use the combination formula \\(\\binom{n}{k}\\), where \\(n\\) is the total number of items and \\(k\\) is the number of items to choose.\n\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{3} = \\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 10\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of ways to choose 3 distinct prime factors from 5 is \\(\\boxed{10}\\)."
