"To find the expected value of a coin flip, we need to calculate the weighted average of the outcomes. The expected value \\( E \\) is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ E = \\sum (P_i \\cdot X_i) \\]\n\nwhere \\( P_i \\) are the probabilities and \\( X_i \\) are the corresponding outcomes.\n\nGiven:\n- Probability of heads \\( P(H) = \\frac{3}{4} \\)\n- Probability of tails \\( P(T) = \\frac{1}{4} \\)\n- Outcome for heads \\( X_H = 2 \\) dollars\n- Outcome for tails \\( X_T = -1 \\) dollar\n\nNow, we calculate the expected value:\n\n\\[ E = \\left( \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot 2 \\right) + \\left( \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot (-1) \\right) \\]\n\n\\[ E = \\frac{6}{4} - \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\n\\[ E = \\frac{5}{4} \\]\n\n\\[ E = 1.25 \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\( 1.25 \\)."
"The binomial coefficient $\\dbinom{1001}{2}$ is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\dbinom{1001}{2} = \\frac{1001!}{2!(1001-2)!} = \\frac{1001!}{2! \\cdot 999!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the common terms in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1001!}{2! \\cdot 999!} = \\frac{1001 \\times 1000 \\times 999!}{2! \\times 999!} = \\frac{1001 \\times 1000}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we perform the division:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1001 \\times 1000}{2} = 1001 \\times 500 = 500500\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $\\dbinom{1001}{2}$ is $\\boxed{500500500}$."
