"First, we need to determine the total number of ways to choose 2 marbles out of 18. This is given by the combination formula:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{18}{2} = \\frac{18!}{2!(18-2)!} = \\frac{18 \\times 17}{2 \\times 1} = 153\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 1 red marble out of 12 and 1 blue marble out of 6. This is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{1} \\times \\binom{6}{1} = 12 \\times 6 = 72\n\\]\n\nThe probability of selecting 1 red marble and 1 blue marble is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of outcomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{72}{153} = \\frac{8}{17}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that one marble is red and one is blue is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{8}{17}}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{8}{17}\\)."
