"We can use the Shoelace Theorem to find the area of quadrilateral $ABCD$. The Shoelace Theorem states that for a polygon with vertices $(x_1, y_1)$, $(x_2, y_2)$, $\\dots$, $(x_n, y_n)$, the area is given by\n\n$$\\frac{1}{2} \\left| \\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} (x_i y_{i+1} - y_i x_{i+1}) + (x_n y_1 - y_n x_1) \\right|.$$\n\nApplying this formula to quadrilateral $ABCD$, we get\n\n$$\\frac{1}{2} \\left| (0\\cdot3 + 1\\cdot2 + 5\\cdot0 + 4\\cdot1) - (1\\cdot1 + 3\\cdot5 + 2\\cdot4 + 0\\cdot0) \\right| = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| (2 + 2 + 0 + 4) - (1 + 15 + 8 + 0) \\right| = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| -6 \\right| = \\boxed{3}.$$"
