"Let the original width, length, and height of the rectangular prism be \\( w \\), \\( l \\), and \\( h \\) respectively. The original volume \\( V \\) of the prism is given by:\n\n\\[ V = w \\times l \\times h \\]\n\nIf each dimension is increased by \\( 10\\% \\), the new dimensions become \\( 1.1w \\), \\( 1.1l \\), and \\( 1.1h \\). The new volume \\( V' \\) of the prism is:\n\n\\[ V' = (1.1w) \\times (1.1l) \\times (1.1h) = 1.331V \\]\n\nThe percent increase in the volume is calculated as:\n\n\\[ \\text{Percent Increase} = \\left( \\frac{V' - V}{V} \\right) \\times 100 = \\left( \\frac{1.331V - V}{V} \\right) \\times 100 = 33.1\\% \\]\n\nTo the nearest whole number, the volume percent increase is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{33\\%} \\]"
"To find the volume of water in the pool, we first need to calculate the total volume of the pool when it is full. The volume \\( V \\) of a rectangular prism (which is the shape of the pool) is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ V = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\times \\text{depth} \\]\n\nGiven the dimensions of the pool:\n- Length = 12 meters\n- Width = 6 meters\n- Depth = 4 meters\n\nSubstitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[ V = 12 \\, \\text{m} \\times 6 \\, \\text{m} \\times 4 \\, \\text{m} \\]\n\\[ V = 288 \\, \\text{m}^3 \\]\n\nSince the pool is half full of water, the volume of water in the pool is half of the total volume:\n\n\\[ \\text{Volume of water} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 288 \\, \\text{m}^3 = 144 \\, \\text{m}^3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the volume of water in the pool is \\(144\\) cubic meters."
