"We start by rewriting the given equation:\n\\[4x^3 - 41x^2 + 10x = 1989.\\]\n\nFirst, we try to factor the left-hand side. Notice that the coefficients of the polynomial are symmetric and can be factored in a specific form. We can use the fact that the polynomial can be factored as:\n\\[4x^3 - 41x^2 + 10x = (2x - 1)(2x^2 - 40x + 10).\\]\n\nNext, we factor the quadratic term:\n\\[2x^2 - 40x + 10 = (2x - 1)(2x - 10).\\]\n\nThus, the equation becomes:\n\\[(2x - 1)(2x - 10) = 1989.\\]\n\nWe can solve this equation by trial and error or by recognizing that 1989 is a prime number. By testing values, we find that:\n\\[x = 10.\\]\n\nTherefore, the positive integer \\(x\\) is:\n\\[\\boxed{10}.\\]"
"To find the coefficient of \\(x^{49}\\) in the expansion of \\((x + 1)(x + 3)(x + 5) \\dotsm (x + 95)(x + 97)(x + 99)\\), we can use the fact that the product of the first \\(n\\) odd numbers is \\(x^n - 1\\). Here, \\(n = 49\\).\n\nThe product of the first \\(n\\) odd numbers is given by:\n\\[\n(x + 1)(x + 3)(x + 5) \\dotsm (x + (2n - 1)) = x^n - 1\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 49\\), we have:\n\\[\n(x + 1)(x + 3)(x + 5) \\dotsm (x + 95)(x + 97)(x + 99) = x^{49} - 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the coefficient of \\(x^{49}\\) in the expansion is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(1\\). I hope it is correct."
