"To calculate \\((.\\overline{6})(3)\\), we first need to express the repeating decimal \\(0.\\overline{6}\\) as a fraction.\n\nLet \\(x = 0.\\overline{6}\\). Then, we can write:\n\\[ x = 0.6666\\ldots \\]\n\nTo eliminate the repeating part, we multiply both sides by 10:\n\\[ 10x = 6.6666\\ldots \\]\n\nNow, we subtract the original \\(x\\) from this equation:\n\\[ 10x - x = 6.6666\\ldots - 0.6666\\ldots \\]\n\\[ 9x = 6 \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{6}{9} \\]\n\nSo, \\(0.\\overline{6} = \\frac{6}{9}\\).\n\nNext, we multiply \\(\\frac{6}{9}\\) by 3:\n\\[ \\frac{6}{9} \\times 3 = \\frac{18}{9} = 2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]"
