"To solve the inequality \\(3(x-5) \\le 7\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. Distribute the 3 on the left side of the inequality:\n   \\[\n   3x - 15 \\le 7\n   \\]\n\n2. Add 15 to both sides of the inequality to isolate the term with \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   3x - 15 + 15 \\le 7 + 15\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   3x \\le 22\n   \\]\n\n3. Divide both sides of the inequality by 3 to solve for \\(x\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3x}{3} \\le \\frac{22}{3}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying this, we get:\n   \\[\n   x \\le 7\n   \\]\n\nThe positive integer solutions to the inequality \\(x \\le 7\\) are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. Therefore, there are \\(\\boxed{6}\\) solutions."
"To find the product of \\(\\frac{-5}{9}\\) and \\(\\frac{8}{17}\\), we multiply the numerators together and the denominators together:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-5}{9} \\cdot \\frac{8}{17} = \\frac{-5 \\cdot 8}{9 \\cdot 17}\n\\]\n\nNext, we perform the multiplication in the numerator:\n\n\\[\n-5 \\cdot 8 = -40\n\\]\n\nThen, we perform the multiplication in the denominator:\n\n\\[\n9 \\cdot 17 = 153\n\\]\n\nSo, the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-40}{153}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify this fraction, we find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 40 and 153, which is 7. We divide both the numerator and the denominator by 7:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{-40 \\div 7}{153 \\div 7} = \\frac{-5}{21}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the product is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{-5}{21}}\n\\]"
