"To find the median number of students in 2005, we first need to list the number of students for each county in 2005. From the table, the number of students in 2005 are:\n\n- Aiken: 136\n- Bamberg: 11\n- Barnwell: 29\n- Berkeley: 524\n- Calhoun: 11\n- Cherokee: 19\n- Chesterfield: 29\n- Colleton: 41\n\nNext, we arrange these numbers in ascending order:\n\n\\[ 11, 11, 19, 29, 29, 41, 136, 524 \\]\n\nSince there are 8 counties, the median will be the average of the 4th and 5th numbers in this ordered list. The 4th number is 29 and the 5th number is 29. Therefore, the median is:\n\n\\[ \\frac{29 + 29}{2} = 29 \\]\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{29}\\)."
"Let the length of the picture be \\( l \\) and the width of the picture be \\( w \\). According to the problem, the area of the picture is 2 square feet, so we have:\n\n\\[ l \\times w = 2 \\]\n\nThe length of the quilt is twice the length of the picture, and the width of the quilt is three times the width of the picture. Therefore, the length of the quilt is \\( 2l \\) and the width of the quilt is \\( 3w \\).\n\nThe area of the quilt is given by the product of its length and width:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area of the quilt} = (2l) \\times (3w) = 6(l \\times w) \\]\n\nSince \\( l \\times w = 2 \\), we substitute this into the equation:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area of the quilt} = 6 \\times 2 = 12 \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the quilt is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{12} \\text{ square feet} \\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\( 12 \\) square feet. I hope it is correct."
"To determine the probability that all four coins (a penny, a nickel, a dime, and a quarter) come up heads when flipped simultaneously, we need to consider the probability of each individual coin showing heads and then multiply these probabilities together.\n\nEach coin has two possible outcomes: heads (H) or tails (T). The probability of getting heads on a single coin flip is \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\nSince the flips of the coins are independent events, the probability of all four coins showing heads is the product of the probabilities of each individual coin showing heads:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^4 = \\frac{1}{16}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that all four coins come up heads is \\( \\boxed{\\frac{1}{16}} \\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{16}}\\)."
"Let the side length of each square be \\( s \\). When three squares are placed side by side, the length of the rectangle is \\( 3s \\) and the width is \\( s \\).\n\nThe perimeter of the rectangle is given by the formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter} = 2 \\times (\\text{length} + \\text{width})\n\\]\nSubstituting the given dimensions:\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter} = 2 \\times (3s + s) = 2 \\times 4s = 8s\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the perimeter is 104 inches:\n\\[\n8s = 104\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( s \\):\n\\[\ns = \\frac{104}{8} = 13\n\\]\n\nThus, the side length of each square is 13 inches. The area of each square is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = s^2 = 13^2 = 169 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of each square is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{169}\n\\]\nsquare inches."
