"To determine how many degrees the temperature will rise in the next 2 hours, we first need to find the rate of temperature increase per minute. We know that the temperature rises by 1.5 degrees in 15 minutes. Therefore, the rate of increase per minute is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1.5 \\text{ degrees}}{15 \\text{ minutes}} = \\frac{1.5}{15} \\text{ degrees per minute} = \\frac{1}{10} \\text{ degrees per minute}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to calculate the total temperature increase over 2 hours. There are 60 minutes in an hour, so 2 hours is:\n\n\\[\n2 \\times 60 = 120 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\nNow, we multiply the rate of increase per minute by the total number of minutes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{10} \\text{ degrees per minute} \\times 120 \\text{ minutes} = 12 \\text{ degrees}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the temperature will rise by \\(\\boxed{12}\\) degrees in the next \\( 2 \\) hours."
"To determine how many prime numbers must be multiplied to make 2400, we first need to find the prime factorization of 2400.\n\n1. Divide 2400 by the smallest prime number, 2:\n   \\[\n   2400 \\div 2 = 1200\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   1200 \\div 2 = 600\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   600 \\div 2 = 300\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   300 \\div 2 = 150\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   150 \\div 2 = 75\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   75 \\div 3 = 25\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   25 \\div 5 = 5\n   \\]\n\nThe prime factorization of 2400 is \\(2^5 \\times 3 \\times 5\\).\n\nTherefore, the number of primes that must be multiplied to make 2400 is \\(\\boxed{6}\\)."
