"We start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\cos 3x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(\\frac{\\sin 3x}{\\sin x}\\). Notice that \\(\\frac{\\sin 3x}{\\sin x}\\) can be rewritten using the identity:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 3x}{\\sin x} = \\frac{4\\cos 3x - 3\\cos x}{\\cos x}\n\\]\n\nUsing the given equation, we substitute \\(\\frac{\\cos 3x}{\\cos x} = \\frac{1}{3}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 3x}{\\sin x} = \\frac{4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} - 3}{\\cos x}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the numerator:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 3x}{\\sin x} = \\frac{4 - 9}{\\cos x} = \\frac{-5}{\\cos x}\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sin 3x}{\\sin x} = -5\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-5}\n\\]"
