"The magnitude of the projection of \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) onto \\(\\mathbf{w}\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\|\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}} \\mathbf{v}\\| = \\left| \\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}}{\\|\\mathbf{w}\\|^2} \\right|\n\\]\n\nGiven \\(\\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = 5\\) and \\(\\|\\mathbf{w}\\| = 8\\), we have:\n\\[\n\\|\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}} \\mathbf{v}\\| = \\left| \\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}}{8^2} \\right| = \\left| \\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}}{64} \\right|\n\\]\n\nThe largest possible value of \\(\\|\\operatorname{proj}_{\\mathbf{w}} \\mathbf{v}\\|\\) occurs when \\(\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{w}\\) is maximized. The maximum value of the dot product of two vectors is 1, so:\n\\[\n\\left| \\frac{1}{64} \\right| = \\frac{1}{64}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the largest possible value is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{64}}\\)."
"We can rewrite the given equation as:\n\\[\\frac{1}{x - \\tan 20^{\\circ}} + \\frac{1}{x + \\tan 40^{\\circ}} + \\frac{1}{x - \\tan 80^{\\circ}} = 0.\\]\nThis equation can be factored as:\n\\[\\frac{1}{x - \\tan 20^{\\circ}} + \\frac{1}{x + \\tan 40^{\\circ}} + \\frac{1}{x - \\tan 80^{\\circ}} = \\frac{1}{x - \\tan 20^{\\circ}} + \\frac{1}{x + \\tan 40^{\\circ}} + \\frac{1}{x - \\tan 80^{\\circ}} = 0.\\]\nTherefore, the solutions to the equation are:\n\\[x = \\tan 20^{\\circ}, x = -\\tan 40^{\\circ}, x = \\tan 80^{\\circ}.\\]\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $\\boxed{\\tan 20^{\\circ}, -\\tan 40^{\\circ}, \\tan 80^{\\circ}}.$ I hope it is correct."
"We start by squaring both sides of the equation $\\sin x + \\cos x = \\frac{1}{5}$:\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2 x + 2\\sin x \\cos x + \\cos^2 x = \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^2\n\\]\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity $\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1$, we get:\n\n\\[\n1 + 2\\sin x \\cos x = \\frac{1}{25}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 1 from both sides, we have:\n\n\\[\n2\\sin x \\cos x = -\\frac{24}{25}\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by 2, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\sin x \\cos x = -\\frac{12}{25}\n\\]\n\nWe know that $\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$, so:\n\n\\[\n\\tan x = \\boxed{-\\frac{12}{25}}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $-\\frac{12}{25}$. I hope it is correct."
"The direction vector of the line $\\ell$ is the same as the normal vector to the plane in which the line $\\ell$ lies. The normal vector to the plane is the negative of the first column of the reflection matrix.\n\nThe reflection matrix is given by:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe first column of this matrix is:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe normal vector to the plane is the negative of this vector:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThus, the direction vector of line $\\ell$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]"
